<div class="spec-line">About</div>
<h1>oh yeah, what <span>about me?</span></h1>

<p class="lede">
  Alright, this is a fun one. What's there to know? Well, if you couldn't tell, i'm a closeted nerd - hence the website i wanted to code* myself. I have a job; so this gives me the privilege of really phoning it in while writing these sections. If i wanted it to be professional i would've kept the ai slop "about me" it vibe-wrote surprisingly accurate about me. 
</p>

<p class="lede">
  Ok what else. I find myself thinking to myself, what goes on the <span>about me</span> tab on a website i'm not publicly advertising or promoting and only giving to my friends who would know more <span>about me</span> or write a better <span>about me</span> than I could probably spitball.
</p>

<p class="lede">
  So the motivation, that's a good one, why ai slop a website. Welp, it was always a medium low priority on the mental to-do list. Kira has one.. I want one <span>about me</span>. So now more than ever when it is the lowest it's been on the to do list (you know cus i'm employed) why now? because I can - and it's easier than ever. why not scratch the itch and get it down and published and whatnots. I have more content, in the way I'd like it (not some un-understandable bad weird HTML CSS baked in crap) from my 20 prompts than I've ever had when working on a website combined. Ai is powerful and it's here to stay. Otherwise, I wanted my launching platform for the lab. I'm semi serious about building cool apps or tools or whatnots, making them all <span>about me</span>, and hosting them out of the lab. That would really scratch the itch. And I wouldn't slop them.. very much, I don't think ai has the gumption to poorly code what I feel like building. 
</p>

<p class="lede">
  Moving along, uhh, lets list things I'm into until the me in <span>about me</span> is long enough - I have no idea if it's too long already, i'm doing this in note pad. I'm into people, and vibing with them - that's an easy one. I like coffee, omg the work coffee is the greatest thing that's ever happened to me. I like the Costco chocolate cake, for no reason at all it is the yummiest moistest thing in the world. I like my family, not just love them i like them too - they're big chillers. I like Games, and all sorts of them. If my social schedule dies down i'm like 8.5/10 excited to play the new black flag reboot. But I also like this surge of party friend slop games, I actually like them and it gets my buddies scattered across Canada (sort of) all together in a (virtual) room wayyy more than if we didn't have them. And they're not competitive games where we have to talk about the game. I can just ask kole "hey, do you ever think about me like that" and see if swings. I like Game of thrones. I like piano, I used to be very good - now i'm shit cus i don't really own one. I like my office coffee. oo I really liked my Europe trip - I acutally might make a gallery here just to commiserate it - oh crap, that might be hard to store the photos, I'm hosting this on a cocktail of free cloud instances, I may need to come up with a more elegant solution to serving photos - hold that thought. Lastly, i like me; all the cool shit <span>about me</span> isn't possible without me, I like Zoltmas and the annual ZoltanBiro 7x7 - I like my job and my hobbies and I like doing this. You call it ego, I call it healthy. ok this seems long enough, I'll hand it in.
</p>

<p class="lede">
  When I'm not at a keyboard, I'm usually out with a camera, drinking
  coffee I definitely didn't need, or losing an afternoon to a hobby I'll
  abandon in three weeks. I like small, well-made things — tools, code,
  or otherwise — and I'm slowly trying to build a life and a career around
  making more of them.
</p>

